# SFT execution status v0.73

2026-09-10. **DCP checkpoint reads are now budgeted in a new CPU-kernel successor,
and tiny BF16/FP32 roundtrips pass.** This completes the DCP-read part of v0.72's
native-I/O dependency, not the entire host guard, real import or Gate 5.

No real weights, trainer/tokenizer payloads or datasets were read. No GPU,
inference, training, network action, dependency installation, instance action,
GitHub publication or CI run was initiated. Previous evidence remains unchanged.

## Implemented and reused

`posttraining_checkpoint_dcp_reader_v1.py` connects the existing frozen
`CheckpointReadSession`, `CheckpointReadView` and worker's instance-local DCP
adapter. It verifies the complete checkpoint manifest and all declared files
before DCP metadata deserialization. The reader's Torch import is lazy, after
that audit; the encompassing conversion kernel still imports ML and creates its
meta-only architecture before entering the reader. Neither operation loads real
inputs in this stage.

Every subsequent DCP metadata/tensor read, including rereads, shares the same
nonrefundable byte limit. The frozen reader reserves bytes before each `pread`,
in chunks no larger than 1 MiB. File-like views enforce bounds and expose neither
`fileno` nor a native pathname. No global Torch/Python file function is patched
by the implementation. The original DCP transforms remain rejected.

`posttraining_checkpoint_import_torch_v4.py` explicitly uses that reader instead
of constructing an unmetered `FileSystemReader`. It accepts an independently
chosen checkpoint-read contract and returns a new v0.4 receipt with aggregate
audit/read counters. AST comparison verifies that the v3 conversion predicates
and helpers remain unchanged apart from the explicit reader scope and receipt
addition. Shape/dtype/chunk coverage, independent tied-alias validation,
nonfinite rejection, parameter-only casts, FP32 buffer preservation and complete
HF reload comparison remain in place. V3 was not edited.

## Integration issue fixed

The installed Torch `load_state_dict` calls `reset()` at the start of its first
load. Denying every reset blocked that legitimate first call. The new adapter
allows exactly one **argument-free, no-op initialization handshake** before
restore; it does not reset the source binding, byte counters or deadline.
A second reset, a reset with arguments, or a second `read_data` invocation fails
terminally. Restore is consumed before entering native code, including failure.

The reader also closes its session and checks the budget after close. A late
deadline/cleanup failure cannot publish success. The kernel preserves only its
own exact typed, allowlisted validation codes through context error scrubbing;
cleanup/read errors take precedence. Raw exception text is never a receipt field.

## Verification

**485 distinct tests pass in the targeted suites, with zero failures or skips.**
This is not a full-repository test claim.

- 414 stdlib tests on Python 3.11.15, 3.13.7 and 3.14.6: 0.777s, 0.808s and
  0.827s. This includes the existing reader/view/metered-I/O/worker tests and
  v0.72's 195-test stdlib regression set.
- 71 genuine tiny CPU tests on Python 3.13.7: 0.758s test time / 2.073s supervised
  wall time under a 60-second parent timeout. The prior 60 native tests pass too.
- 48 new tests: 30 reader, seven source/contract and 11 native integration.
- Exact Ruff E,F,I,B,UP / py311 / line length 100 passes on all five new code/test
  files. Existing Torch DCP/platform warnings remain visible.

Native tests verify both BF16 and FP32 roundtrips, reject incorrect hashes before
pickle, exhaust the shared read budget after the initial audit, preserve tied/
nonfinite checks, and successfully load with the stock DCP path stream disabled.
The reported byte count exactly matches intercepted `read`/`pread` request sizes
for the tiny checkpoint. Tensor deserialization receives descriptor-free bounded
views with CPU/weights-only loading. The old packager explicitly rejects the new
kernel receipt rather than silently treating it as accepted v3 evidence.

Evidence: `evidence/lumi-4b-sft-import-metered-dcp-v0.1.json`.
Structural verification:
`evidence/lumi-4b-sft-import-metered-dcp-verification-v0.1.json`.

## Remaining work, without changing existing limits

This is **logical content-byte accounting**, not physical device/page-cache
measurement or an OS sandbox. DCP metadata still uses pickle: hash integrity is
not safe unpickling or origin authentication. Inputs must be trusted and quiescent.
The reused session requires existing 0600 files and 0700 directories; it refuses
0400 files without changing their permissions. Real input compatibility has not
been established or worked around with chmod.

HF `save_pretrained`/`from_pretrained`, small config/runtime-code reads and hard
RAM/write/network/runtime containment remain outside this new DCP ledger. The
next software task is to mediate those HF/control I/O paths, then explicitly
version coordinator/package integration and its aggregate read accounting.
This reader performs a full audit of its own; plugging it into v0.72 unchanged
would add an audit. That is **not** an approved increase to the existing two-audit
plan: integration must account for/reuse the verified audit or obtain an explicit
new bound, never silently raise limits. The old coordinator, package and real
resource plan are unchanged and are not yet wired to this successor.

Existing Linux cgroup/guardian components cover RAM/swap/processes and owned-group
deadline/parent-loss teardown. Their actual integration and native qualification,
plus filesystem/network/write constraints and exact host/runtime admission, are
still required. Mac tests do not qualify those Linux controls. No GPU is needed
for the remaining importer software work; the real `run_import` entry stays
closed. Gate 5, real SFT and production eligibility remain false.

The downstream GPU worker/profile, data relocation and CUDA/smoke dependencies
remain as recorded in v0.70-v0.72. No base, dataset, rights or evaluation rule was
changed. HEAD remains `ecc545fc423650bf1089311915290523c10b3172`, with unchanged
tracked/staged diffs. This stage adds only two modules, three test files and the
report, structural verification and status successor, all local/unpublished.
